          "id": "discover",
          "op": "toolbar",
          "spec": {
            "icon": "map",
            "label": "Scan a network for Hosts",
            "run": "discover",
            "text": "Scan"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "discover",
          "op": "help",
          "spec": {
            "markdown": "# Discover\n\nScans a network for Hosts, from this computer, the way `roster discover` does, and shows what answered: its address, the name it would get, what it looks like, its open ports and its web page's title. You keep the ones you want and add them: they go into the Inventory in a new group, **unreviewed**, as Pending changes you review in `:changes` and Publish. Nothing is added, and nothing connects to a Host, before you say so.\n\n- `:discover 10.0.0.0/24` scans a range (several: `:discover 10.0.0.0/24 10.0.1.0/28`). It asks first, with how many addresses and ports.\n- `:discover` opens the view; `␣ y d` and the toolbar's **Scan** do the same.\n- On a row: `m` keeps it, `u` doesn't, `A` adds the kept ones, `i` or Enter shows its details. `:discover keep all` and `:discover keep none` mark every new one.\n- `:discover stop` (or `:cancel scan`, or the stop button by the progress) stops the scan; what it found stays.\n\nThe scan runs in roster serve: nmap when it's installed (service versions and web page titles), else roster's own TCP connect scan. Up to 4,096 addresses as asked; more, up to a /16, only after you type the range. A Host already in the Inventory shows with its name, greyed out.\n\nIt needs `serve:discover.plan`, `.start`, `.status` and `.stop` to scan, and `serve:discover.add` to add. See `:help plugins`.",
            "summary": "Scan a network from the app and add what it finds",
            "title": "Discover"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "discover",
          "op": "health",
          "spec": {
            "check": "fn",
            "title": "Discover"
          }
        },
